c{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535442 true] [17592186349344 1017 #edb/ref 17592186349345 13194139535442 true] [17592186349344 1089 "q-122314" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349344 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535442 true] [17592186349344 1100 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=3x\\,m/s.$ If $x=2\\,m$ when $t=0,$ find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349344 1113 #uuid "7dbc5a35-d0eb-5e89-8cd9-f0c37eba2581" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349344 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064798/assets/efdb69673b4088d3c021d78282642ff7abc57158e23704cd3d5e7ff2559cbdbb.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3x \\\\ \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & =t+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|x| & =3t+3K \\\\ x & ={e}^{3t+3K} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=2$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=2,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{3t}.\n$$" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349346 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349347 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349348 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349349 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1000 #edb/ref 17592186349350 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1041 "selection" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1104 #edb/ref 17592186349348 13194139535442 true] [17592186349345 1110 #uuid "6647d8d8-bf81-5408-bed4-efaaf0b0ca87" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349346 1034 "x(t)=3{e}^{2t}" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535442 true] [17592186349346 1111 #uuid "5e485a42-fc6c-5a8b-82c6-be7ffa13ca50" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349347 1034 "x(t)=2{e}^{3{t}^{2}}" 13194139535442 true] [17592186349347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535442 true] [17592186349347 1111 #uuid "e7348bfd-2e57-5b03-be06-88093374b8